1
00:00:17,087 --> 00:00:19,208
[SPEAKER_03]: What is a relinquishment?

2
00:00:19,268 --> 00:00:19,788
[SPEAKER_03]: Giving up.

3
00:00:21,689 --> 00:00:22,650
[SPEAKER_03]: What do you need to give up?

4
00:00:23,310 --> 00:00:23,630
[SPEAKER_03]: Why?

5
00:00:24,551 --> 00:00:25,171
[SPEAKER_03]: What does it mean?

6
00:00:26,126 --> 00:00:26,966
[SPEAKER_03]: Why is it necessary?

7
00:00:27,827 --> 00:00:29,048
[SPEAKER_03]: So, what... what practically?

8
00:00:29,228 --> 00:00:36,071
[SPEAKER_03]: What are the things you have to give up, which you actually step to, trying to relinquish, forsake?

9
00:00:36,952 --> 00:00:38,192
[SPEAKER_03]: Trying to manage the suffering.

10
00:00:39,173 --> 00:00:39,473
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay.

11
00:00:39,493 --> 00:00:50,999
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if you're not trying to manage suffering, but you still live with your spouse, children, can you keep that and then give up management and be an arahant, enlightened, free from suffering?

12
00:00:51,339 --> 00:00:51,819
[SPEAKER_03]: Probably not.

13
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:54,161
[SPEAKER_03]: So, there are certain things you have to give up, give up.

14
00:00:54,981 --> 00:00:57,823
[SPEAKER_03]: So, where's the line with the things you have to give up, give up?

15
00:00:59,704 --> 00:01:07,568
[SPEAKER_02]: Maybe you should try to understand what things you don't really need, or you can live without them, and then... Yeah, that's a good, yeah.

16
00:01:08,109 --> 00:01:12,191
[SPEAKER_03]: Basically, re-evaluate, like, what is necessary to have.

17
00:01:14,155 --> 00:01:14,595
[SPEAKER_03]: What isn't?

18
00:01:15,476 --> 00:01:16,156
[SPEAKER_03]: And then start there.

19
00:01:16,276 --> 00:01:26,383
[SPEAKER_03]: But obviously, sometimes, that can result, not that specifically, but people can have a view in regards to that whereby they think, okay, so if I give everything up, I'll be free.

20
00:01:27,520 --> 00:01:34,844
[SPEAKER_03]: So, they don't use any discernment or reason to see where the line is, and just start throwing everything away, so to speak.

21
00:01:35,985 --> 00:01:44,989
[SPEAKER_03]: And, yeah, that might result in a very simple life, but fundamentally, you cannot do that with your own body and your own senses, and that's where the problem is.

22
00:01:45,069 --> 00:01:49,532
[SPEAKER_03]: You can't just... just discard it, like you can items that you possess.

23
00:01:51,840 --> 00:01:54,882
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that needs to be understood, while it is still there.

24
00:01:55,362 --> 00:02:06,146
[SPEAKER_03]: So, while you still have it, you need to understand how... how you can not have it, really, without destroying it, without blaming the eyes or ears for hearing or seeing, and so on.

25
00:02:07,687 --> 00:02:11,289
[SPEAKER_00]: People often think, if I like something, I should give it up.

26
00:02:12,529 --> 00:02:15,290
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for example, if... You should give up my likes.

27
00:02:15,731 --> 00:02:21,073
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, whatever food is my preference, for example, I should stop eating that food.

28
00:02:21,422 --> 00:02:21,642
[SPEAKER_03]: Hmm.

29
00:02:22,482 --> 00:02:23,423
[SPEAKER_00]: Is that all valid?

30
00:02:24,903 --> 00:02:25,144
[SPEAKER_03]: Is it?

31
00:02:26,284 --> 00:02:26,464
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

32
00:02:30,486 --> 00:02:34,847
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's not what the problem is.

33
00:02:34,947 --> 00:02:40,930
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is not that something is agreeable to you, because you've... if things are...

34
00:02:42,390 --> 00:02:50,934
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is that you can't see the line between something being agreeable and you needing to have it and needing to keep it and wanting it.

35
00:02:52,022 --> 00:02:55,144
[SPEAKER_00]: Or being enslaved by it, or being dependent on it.

36
00:02:56,385 --> 00:03:04,369
[SPEAKER_00]: So, just because something is agreeable to you, that you have a preference for something, doesn't mean you have to relinquish it.

37
00:03:04,749 --> 00:03:06,390
[SPEAKER_03]: But what if you're emotionally attached to it?

38
00:03:07,831 --> 00:03:11,533
[SPEAKER_03]: What if you really... you're gonna be miserable if you don't get what you like.

39
00:03:13,294 --> 00:03:14,255
[SPEAKER_03]: Should you give it up then?

40
00:03:15,422 --> 00:03:22,106
[SPEAKER_00]: So, sometimes, I think, yes, it can help, but then, there's obviously a limit to that.

41
00:03:22,146 --> 00:03:29,571
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you can't give up all food, because you can't... just because you can't stop having some kind of desire in regard to food.

42
00:03:29,611 --> 00:03:34,754
[SPEAKER_03]: But would it be okay that somebody gives up only food that they like, and only stuff that they don't like?

43
00:03:36,195 --> 00:03:40,057
[SPEAKER_03]: Would that result in growing their mind?

44
00:03:40,417 --> 00:03:43,139
[SPEAKER_03]: And... No.

45
00:03:43,799 --> 00:03:44,180
[SPEAKER_03]: Why not?

46
00:03:45,771 --> 00:03:50,856
[SPEAKER_03]: Because it's still acting out of likes and dislikes, but just denying, just doing the opposite.

47
00:03:52,317 --> 00:03:54,219
[SPEAKER_03]: Right, but they're not feeding their likes anymore.

48
00:03:54,699 --> 00:03:56,120
[SPEAKER_03]: They're not eating the stuff they like.

49
00:03:56,440 --> 00:03:57,922
[SPEAKER_03]: They only eat the stuff that they don't like.

50
00:03:58,402 --> 00:04:00,224
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, but they're acting out of the same thing.

51
00:04:00,744 --> 00:04:02,226
[SPEAKER_02]: Which thing?

52
00:04:02,286 --> 00:04:02,706
[SPEAKER_02]: Preference.

53
00:04:04,988 --> 00:04:07,590
[SPEAKER_02]: Just turning it the other way.

54
00:04:07,650 --> 00:04:09,452
[SPEAKER_03]: The preference towards the liking food?

55
00:04:09,472 --> 00:04:09,892
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes, the taste.

56
00:04:10,333 --> 00:04:10,733
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, sure.

57
00:04:11,494 --> 00:04:12,795
[SPEAKER_03]: And what else is a problem there?

58
00:04:14,232 --> 00:04:15,552
[SPEAKER_03]: The belief that the problem is...

59
00:04:16,033 --> 00:04:17,073
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly, yeah.

60
00:04:17,713 --> 00:04:22,314
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, your preference, your like, your emotional dependence was not in the taste.

61
00:04:23,575 --> 00:04:35,898
[SPEAKER_03]: So, yes, there is a practical line there, as in, if you are, if you have no perspective, then, yeah, maybe give it up, like, don't eat that, just to see whether your mind will be disturbed and how much.

62
00:04:36,818 --> 00:04:39,539
[SPEAKER_03]: But fundamentally, yeah, that is not where the problem is.

63
00:04:40,119 --> 00:04:44,701
[SPEAKER_03]: The problem is in the desire and lust that's infecting your thoughts, not the sense objects.

64
00:04:47,182 --> 00:04:51,123
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's where the uprooting, that's where the true relinquishment happens.

65
00:04:52,224 --> 00:05:01,067
[SPEAKER_03]: But in order to even kind of comprehend that direction, yeah, some practical physical relinquishment needs to be done, needs to be actually performed.

66
00:05:02,072 --> 00:05:08,376
[SPEAKER_03]: So, yeah, the reference is, of course, the precepts, things you would give up through the precepts.

67
00:05:08,636 --> 00:05:16,141
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, further than that, yeah, you can, you know, everybody can get really inspired with the idea of the austere life and then just start throwing everything out.

68
00:05:16,322 --> 00:05:18,803
[SPEAKER_03]: But now, that can become an issue.

69
00:05:19,223 --> 00:05:25,468
[SPEAKER_03]: You might be just acting out of a view that implies that problem was in these things, not in my desire and lust on the mental level.

70
00:05:26,580 --> 00:05:30,623
[SPEAKER_03]: But if, on the other hand, you use some practical kind of reasoning and say, well, do I really need this?

71
00:05:31,384 --> 00:05:32,385
[SPEAKER_03]: Can I live without it?

72
00:05:33,406 --> 00:05:35,127
[SPEAKER_03]: Certainly, it's better to be less cluttered.

73
00:05:35,207 --> 00:05:40,451
[SPEAKER_03]: Certainly, it's better to go in the direction of relinquishment and simplification, but... And then, then fine.

74
00:05:40,471 --> 00:05:45,255
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, if you decide the things can be dismissed, given up, given away, that's... that's all right.

75
00:05:45,335 --> 00:05:54,723
[SPEAKER_03]: But you still keep the perspective that the... that the problem is actually fundamentally on the level of the mind taking up those things, the mind taking up the...

76
00:05:56,347 --> 00:06:00,169
[SPEAKER_03]: The preference is with lust, leaning towards them, with lust, with aversion and so on.

77
00:06:01,710 --> 00:06:02,990
[SPEAKER_03]: What kind of relinquishment then?

78
00:06:03,010 --> 00:06:13,255
[SPEAKER_03]: Because the suttas say basically that pretty much the prerequisite for establishing a mind and dwelling in jhāna is the complete relinquishment.

79
00:06:14,695 --> 00:06:20,818
[SPEAKER_03]: And even the... like even satipaṭṭhāna, which is generally the practice of mindfulness, presupposes

80
00:06:22,353 --> 00:06:28,878
[SPEAKER_03]: Overcoming happiness and concerns in regard to the entire world, and then you can dwell mindfully.

81
00:06:30,439 --> 00:06:32,741
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's also a form of relinquishment.

82
00:06:34,943 --> 00:06:35,643
[SPEAKER_03]: To what extent?

83
00:06:36,964 --> 00:06:38,365
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean, I don't need to ask to what extent.

84
00:06:38,405 --> 00:06:38,946
[SPEAKER_03]: I just said it.

85
00:06:39,286 --> 00:06:40,087
[SPEAKER_03]: The entire world.

86
00:06:40,807 --> 00:06:41,688
[SPEAKER_03]: But what would be the...

87
00:06:42,849 --> 00:06:44,550
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay, enough.

88
00:06:45,250 --> 00:06:47,212
[SPEAKER_03]: What would be the practical examples of

89
00:06:49,816 --> 00:06:54,922
[SPEAKER_03]: Giving up and overcoming happiness and relinquishment in regard to the entire world.

90
00:06:55,202 --> 00:07:00,148
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you keep the precepts, you're not engaging with sensuality, but there is still obviously more to the world.

91
00:07:01,129 --> 00:07:06,555
[SPEAKER_03]: What are the other things you have to give up practically, not just metaphorically?

92
00:07:07,878 --> 00:07:24,828
[SPEAKER_03]: In order to be able to surmount the world, to surmount the concern regarding the world, in order to be able to dwell correctly mindful, in order to experience that vowsagā that suttas talk about, that then you are truly detached from the realm of thinking and pondering, the mind is withdrawn from it and that is the first jhāna.

93
00:07:25,929 --> 00:07:27,470
[SPEAKER_03]: What are those things you have to give up?

94
00:07:29,631 --> 00:07:36,355
[SPEAKER_03]: It's obviously coarse sensuality, coarse ill-will, chore, indulgency food, of course, company, of course, but

95
00:07:37,991 --> 00:07:38,531
[SPEAKER_03]: What else now?

96
00:07:40,011 --> 00:07:41,312
[SPEAKER_03]: There's more to that, obviously.

97
00:07:44,232 --> 00:07:57,634
[SPEAKER_02]: Maybe it's, you know, like, indulgence in thought, I'd say, like, to put too much value on, sort of, arranging things.

98
00:07:58,454 --> 00:08:06,636
[SPEAKER_02]: If you see that this ultimately is futile and, I mean, and recognizing that there's a hindrance.

99
00:08:08,590 --> 00:08:10,591
[SPEAKER_02]: You mean like arranging things on the level of the thought?

100
00:08:11,191 --> 00:08:11,431
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes.

101
00:08:11,712 --> 00:08:14,653
[SPEAKER_03]: Sort of controlling it or making it whatever?

102
00:08:15,174 --> 00:08:19,536
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, sort of, yeah, quieten it.

103
00:08:20,037 --> 00:08:24,520
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, but see, the problem with that could be that somebody can do that before they got the right view.

104
00:08:25,981 --> 00:08:29,423
[SPEAKER_03]: So, they're quieting their mind down with ignorance.

105
00:08:29,983 --> 00:08:33,846
[SPEAKER_03]: So, they still don't know the escape, but they start valuing the non-disturbance.

106
00:08:34,746 --> 00:08:37,648
[SPEAKER_03]: But seeking the practice is disturbing.

107
00:08:38,063 --> 00:08:42,646
[SPEAKER_03]: But it's a good type of disturbance, because it can result in the right view, that can then result in complete liberation.

108
00:08:42,666 --> 00:08:54,875
[SPEAKER_03]: That was the... we spoke about it relatively often, the... when somebody... and even the Buddha himself said, in that... the two types of thought, like you... you would first realize how the problem is the unwholesome thoughts, not thought in itself.

109
00:08:55,696 --> 00:08:57,297
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, he put them into two categories.

110
00:08:57,417 --> 00:09:00,139
[SPEAKER_03]: Anything that's greed, aversion, delusion, he would not engage with.

111
00:09:00,300 --> 00:09:03,022
[SPEAKER_03]: Anything that's greed... non-greed, non-aversion, delusion, he would allow.

112
00:09:03,742 --> 00:09:16,567
[SPEAKER_03]: And when greed, aversion, delusion present themselves, he would not like trying to get rid of it, get rid of it, but he would just remind himself of the context, of the peril, of the danger, of how little satisfaction, how this is intending for his harm, for harm of others, for harm of all.

113
00:09:17,187 --> 00:09:18,868
[SPEAKER_03]: Then those thoughts would subside on their own.

114
00:09:20,048 --> 00:09:21,109
[SPEAKER_03]: And that was his practice.

115
00:09:21,329 --> 00:09:23,270
[SPEAKER_03]: And that was not peaceful.

116
00:09:23,610 --> 00:09:24,430
[SPEAKER_03]: That was still work.

117
00:09:24,570 --> 00:09:28,992
[SPEAKER_03]: That was still burden, obviously on the right level of enduring these thoughts pressuring you.

118
00:09:29,774 --> 00:09:34,620
[SPEAKER_03]: Not acting out, and then re-establishing the context in regard to them, until they subside.

119
00:09:35,881 --> 00:09:45,072
[SPEAKER_03]: When he fully developed that, completely, sort of, when all he had was the good, non-greed, non-aversion, non-delusion,

120
00:09:46,106 --> 00:09:51,789
[SPEAKER_03]: He was still maintaining the context, and then he realized, oh, but yeah, I don't need to do this now, because these thoughts are not harmful.

121
00:09:52,390 --> 00:09:53,931
[SPEAKER_03]: So, what if I just don't let them be?

122
00:09:54,911 --> 00:09:59,694
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, and by the way, there was that difference in that Madhyamāgama, in that sutta.

123
00:10:00,194 --> 00:10:02,656
[SPEAKER_03]: In Pali, it doesn't really... it just goes into second jhāna.

124
00:10:03,136 --> 00:10:11,501
[SPEAKER_03]: But this explains how he was first intentionally involved with maintaining the context and doing that bhāvan in regard to the unwholesome states, until they subside.

125
00:10:13,342 --> 00:10:22,625
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, after that, when he had wholesome states endure, he realized, oh, I don't need to be intentionally involved with this context constantly, because these thoughts are not contradicting it, not going against it.

126
00:10:23,265 --> 00:10:33,269
[SPEAKER_03]: And then he, oh, what if I allow these thinking and pondering basically to be, and instead just unify my mind internally on the basis of this wholesome environment that I have worked for?

127
00:10:34,629 --> 00:10:36,250
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, he entered the first jhāna.

128
00:10:37,105 --> 00:10:38,706
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, furthermore, then goes into second.

129
00:10:38,867 --> 00:10:42,330
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that was like missing step I was talking about, that you wouldn't find in the Pali Sutta.

130
00:10:42,850 --> 00:10:43,531
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's the point.

131
00:10:43,571 --> 00:10:45,412
[SPEAKER_03]: So, like...

132
00:10:45,733 --> 00:10:55,001
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes, you shouldn't be... or, like, you know, if somebody is trying to control the thoughts and make any plan, yeah, you've got to recognize that it's not going to lead to a solution.

133
00:10:55,421 --> 00:10:57,203
[SPEAKER_03]: But, at the same time, thoughts are not a problem.

134
00:10:57,823 --> 00:10:59,805
[SPEAKER_03]: First jhāna is thinking and pondering entirely.

135
00:11:00,922 --> 00:11:06,867
[SPEAKER_03]: And it's the peaceful, sublime abiding that's sufficient for arahantship, when the mind is thoroughly withdrawn from it.

136
00:11:07,988 --> 00:11:12,691
[SPEAKER_03]: So, the problem is there, whether those thoughts allude to greed, aversion, delusion, distraction, cruelty, and so on.

137
00:11:13,452 --> 00:11:26,382
[SPEAKER_03]: And those are now subtle things, that I was leading to, by asking, what are the other less subtle things you need to give up, before you can start discerning these roots of your thoughts, in regard to the world.

138
00:11:27,082 --> 00:11:29,164
[SPEAKER_03]: So, given the core sensuality is gone,

139
00:11:30,481 --> 00:11:34,504
[SPEAKER_03]: You know, cruelty, acting out of anger is gone.

140
00:11:34,564 --> 00:11:35,744
[SPEAKER_03]: What are the things that are not gone?

141
00:11:35,945 --> 00:11:40,327
[SPEAKER_01]: There's worldly gain, renown, status, position...

142
00:11:40,347 --> 00:11:48,993
[SPEAKER_03]: So, basically, kind of, your relationship with others, ideas, connections, comparisons... Gone.

143
00:11:49,673 --> 00:11:59,600
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I was going to suggest that it would then come down to the level of views, relinquishing the dependence on

144
00:12:02,071 --> 00:12:06,273
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it would come down to different types of wrong views in regard to the world.

145
00:12:06,913 --> 00:12:14,477
[SPEAKER_03]: So, how would one practically relinquish views in regard to the world, relinquish dependences on the notions of others and so on?

146
00:12:14,577 --> 00:12:15,977
[SPEAKER_03]: What do you practically need to do?

147
00:12:16,598 --> 00:12:19,659
[SPEAKER_03]: Can you just start thinking about those things and relinquish them by figuring them out?

148
00:12:20,319 --> 00:12:25,682
[SPEAKER_03]: Or you do need to increase that certain level of withdrawal from certain things?

149
00:12:25,782 --> 00:12:30,444
[SPEAKER_03]: In the same sense, precepts withdraw you from certain unwholesome behaviors by body and speech.

150
00:12:32,342 --> 00:12:39,864
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, it seems to me that this desire for gain and renown and status and position is all bound up with self-view as well.

151
00:12:40,184 --> 00:12:51,626
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, you know, there's got to be a self-image here, or a notion of self here, which then, you know, will... and then there will be others, and then there's this comparison, better, worse, and all the rest of it.

152
00:12:51,646 --> 00:12:53,147
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, that's all kind of the result of it.

153
00:12:53,567 --> 00:12:57,187
[SPEAKER_03]: But as I said, in order to come to that, which is a problem, what do you need to give up?

154
00:12:58,828 --> 00:12:59,728
[SPEAKER_01]: Your household life.

155
00:13:02,429 --> 00:13:06,411
[SPEAKER_03]: In a way, yeah, more specifically, so if you... Or delighting in company.

156
00:13:07,091 --> 00:13:07,531
[SPEAKER_03]: There you go.

157
00:13:07,691 --> 00:13:11,193
[SPEAKER_03]: So, basically, delighting in company, as in...

158
00:13:11,513 --> 00:13:13,614
[SPEAKER_00]: But what does it mean to delight in company?

159
00:13:13,634 --> 00:13:24,279
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, we can get to that as well, actually, because it doesn't just mean... Because people can think, oh, Buddha said seclusion, and I'm always in seclusion, but the real reason is because I really have ill will towards everybody else.

160
00:13:25,326 --> 00:13:27,988
[SPEAKER_03]: And it's like, well, that's not the seclusion, because you're not secluded.

161
00:13:28,048 --> 00:13:35,272
[SPEAKER_03]: But when I say the true seclusion, like, the one dwells in company even when he's alone, if his mind is not free from greed, ill-will and delusion.

162
00:13:36,392 --> 00:13:42,236
[SPEAKER_03]: So, yes, physical seclusion is necessary, but the reason why you do it is even more necessary, more important.

163
00:13:42,836 --> 00:13:52,642
[SPEAKER_03]: So, physical withdrawal, not seeking company, not finding delight in company, kind of, yeah, that would diminish you referencing yourself from the point of view of company and others.

164
00:13:53,682 --> 00:13:57,105
[SPEAKER_03]: You're distracting yourself from the, again, point of view of company.

165
00:13:57,966 --> 00:14:01,088
[SPEAKER_03]: What, what other things in regard to the world, like this?

166
00:14:01,208 --> 00:14:03,470
[SPEAKER_03]: How would you abandon concerns in regard to the world?

167
00:14:03,570 --> 00:14:04,411
[SPEAKER_03]: What do you need to give up?

168
00:14:05,252 --> 00:14:08,574
[SPEAKER_03]: What makes you concerned regarding the entire world and people and so on?

169
00:14:10,696 --> 00:14:14,159
[SPEAKER_03]: So, we're talking about giving up sensual behavior, ill-will.

170
00:14:15,780 --> 00:14:16,080
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay.

171
00:14:16,581 --> 00:14:19,923
[SPEAKER_03]: What else you do on the level of your senses with the world?

172
00:14:20,644 --> 00:14:22,105
[SPEAKER_03]: How are you engaging with the world?

173
00:14:23,160 --> 00:14:26,522
[SPEAKER_03]: So, through company, what are the things?

174
00:14:27,503 --> 00:14:33,666
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I suppose, seeking... information about it, seeking...

175
00:14:33,726 --> 00:14:34,707
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

176
00:14:35,087 --> 00:14:49,055
[SPEAKER_03]: Engaging with the level of information, news, distraction, views on the level of ideals that pertain to regard to the world, the world, better place, all of that needs to be abandoned, just to begin satipaṭṭhāna rightly.

177
00:14:50,216 --> 00:14:52,437
[SPEAKER_03]: Because those are all factual concerns.

178
00:14:53,798 --> 00:14:58,782
[SPEAKER_03]: They might not be apparent to somebody who is also, like, crowded with sensuality and household life and so on.

179
00:14:59,403 --> 00:15:02,685
[SPEAKER_03]: Once you withdraw yourself from that, yeah, that's what the mind will revolve around.

180
00:15:02,785 --> 00:15:04,347
[SPEAKER_03]: Those are the underlying bases there.

181
00:15:05,067 --> 00:15:21,261
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's why even, like, the... when Buddha describes a monk, obviously, with the right view, says a skilled monk, a noble disciple, abandons... having been withdrawn physically from these... sensuality, aversion, ill-will, he abandons thoughts.

182
00:15:21,928 --> 00:15:25,291
[SPEAKER_03]: ...of sensuality, thoughts of cruelty, thoughts of harming.

183
00:15:26,332 --> 00:15:29,995
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, he abandons, like, their kind of conceited course.

184
00:15:30,496 --> 00:15:36,441
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, he also abandons thoughts of his own, like, clanhood and homeland.

185
00:15:36,561 --> 00:15:41,106
[SPEAKER_03]: And basically, on the level of now, that's where your conceit is, who you are in that broader sense.

186
00:15:41,246 --> 00:15:45,470
[SPEAKER_03]: So, pretty much concerning, you know, the level of the world as us humans.

187
00:15:46,188 --> 00:15:47,549
[SPEAKER_03]: He abandons even those thoughts.

188
00:15:48,110 --> 00:15:51,753
[SPEAKER_03]: By, again, not by denying them, not by trying to get rid of them, but by...

189
00:15:51,773 --> 00:15:53,995
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay, I have those thoughts now enduring in this mind.

190
00:15:54,355 --> 00:15:58,319
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, let me establish the same context I was established for when it came to sensuality.

191
00:15:58,379 --> 00:16:02,583
[SPEAKER_03]: These thoughts lead to more conceit, more suffering, more burden.

192
00:16:02,623 --> 00:16:09,589
[SPEAKER_03]: Whenever there is identity, whether traces of identity or full-blown identity, dukkha will find a footing.

193
00:16:09,809 --> 00:16:11,671
[SPEAKER_03]: Thus, by thinking these thoughts,

194
00:16:12,251 --> 00:16:14,332
[SPEAKER_03]: I am not inclining this mind towards peace.

195
00:16:14,372 --> 00:16:17,674
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm inclining it towards harm, towards myself, towards others, towards everything else.

196
00:16:19,055 --> 00:16:19,835
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's that.

197
00:16:19,955 --> 00:16:21,336
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you have to give up.

198
00:16:21,436 --> 00:16:24,198
[SPEAKER_03]: And you wouldn't be able to give up these thoughts if you're still

199
00:16:25,462 --> 00:16:48,668
[SPEAKER_03]: In company of others, spending your time talking about politics and kings and worlds and ministers, as the suttas would mention, well, we have the present equivalent of that, reading news, reading information, keeping in touch with with happenings, even if not necessarily, you know, political or something, but just, oh, in the world of science, in the world of art, in the world of... in the world of, full stop.

200
00:16:50,328 --> 00:16:53,549
[SPEAKER_03]: Means you still have certain connections to it internally.

201
00:16:54,619 --> 00:16:56,320
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's truly where the problem is.

202
00:16:56,640 --> 00:17:03,324
[SPEAKER_03]: And for as long as those things are not given up on that external level, these thoughts will not be seen for what they are, the subtle ones.

203
00:17:04,245 --> 00:17:05,766
[SPEAKER_03]: And even the duty towards the family.

204
00:17:06,706 --> 00:17:12,009
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean, the whole point of the homeless life was leaving it all behind.

205
00:17:12,049 --> 00:17:18,253
[SPEAKER_03]: So, like, you know, many monks would be kind of, oh, but it's still, it's my duty towards family, my own family.

206
00:17:19,062 --> 00:17:33,195
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like, well, to that extent you will not be abandoning thoughts of your clanshold and belonging and caste and wherever you come from, because you are still taking on that external duty through which you are maintaining this internal identity.

207
00:17:34,836 --> 00:17:35,317
[SPEAKER_03]: It has to be.

208
00:17:35,437 --> 00:17:40,141
[SPEAKER_03]: I am alone in this world for which I have no more happiness nor concern.

209
00:17:41,818 --> 00:17:55,434
[SPEAKER_00]: And the reason that you would have no more happiness or concern in regard to the world, is precisely because you would see also that, for example, you know, if you get bothered by

210
00:17:56,432 --> 00:18:01,354
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, war going on in the world, or poverty going on in the world, or things going on.

211
00:18:01,774 --> 00:18:04,315
[SPEAKER_00]: But you see, what is the root cause of any of those things?

212
00:18:04,595 --> 00:18:06,375
[SPEAKER_00]: Greed, aversion, delusion, for example.

213
00:18:06,736 --> 00:18:12,678
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you're taking... if you see where that problem is, rightly, in yourself... You couldn't be concerned.

214
00:18:12,698 --> 00:18:16,239
[SPEAKER_00]: You could not be concerned whatsoever with anything that goes on with the world.

215
00:18:16,259 --> 00:18:17,539
[SPEAKER_00]: You're taking that seriously.

216
00:18:17,959 --> 00:18:19,560
[SPEAKER_03]: That is why you're concerned with the world.

217
00:18:19,580 --> 00:18:25,482
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly, because you are not... Because of those things that are root of all these problems and concerns and seeking the safety and so on.

218
00:18:27,847 --> 00:18:30,369
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that needs to be relinquished as well.

219
00:18:30,389 --> 00:18:42,080
[SPEAKER_01]: There's that obviously mundane right view and that wrong view that says there is no mother, there's no father, there's no world, no next world, nothing.

220
00:18:42,100 --> 00:18:43,542
[SPEAKER_03]: No next world, there's nothing, there's no given.

221
00:18:44,002 --> 00:18:45,704
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, it doesn't really exist.

222
00:18:45,744 --> 00:18:47,806
[SPEAKER_01]: You see, I'm not identified with any of those things.

223
00:18:48,546 --> 00:18:49,147
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, it's right.

224
00:18:49,748 --> 00:18:50,648
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's actually the wrong.

225
00:18:50,869 --> 00:18:51,469
[SPEAKER_03]: It's upside down.

226
00:18:51,569 --> 00:18:51,769
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

227
00:18:53,528 --> 00:18:55,690
[SPEAKER_01]: There is mother, there is father, there is family.

228
00:18:56,330 --> 00:18:58,672
[SPEAKER_03]: And you have overcome the concern in regard to it.

229
00:18:59,292 --> 00:19:04,076
[SPEAKER_03]: Not that you have denied it, or got rid of them, or threw them out, like you would throw your possessions.

230
00:19:04,896 --> 00:19:05,557
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's the point.

231
00:19:05,637 --> 00:19:06,037
[SPEAKER_03]: That's what I mean.

232
00:19:06,057 --> 00:19:09,920
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, oh, so you can't throw out the world.

233
00:19:09,960 --> 00:19:11,261
[SPEAKER_03]: You can't throw out your senses.

234
00:19:11,441 --> 00:19:15,844
[SPEAKER_03]: Or, well, you can, but you would still equally be bound by those domains, by those consciousness.

235
00:19:16,124 --> 00:19:16,685
[SPEAKER_03]: You're not free.

236
00:19:17,805 --> 00:19:21,408
[SPEAKER_03]: What you can do is overcome that basis.

237
00:19:23,113 --> 00:19:27,055
[SPEAKER_03]: For concern, for lust, for aversion, for distraction, for needing the safety.

238
00:19:28,156 --> 00:19:31,958
[SPEAKER_03]: The basis, basically, that's keeping your mind from being liberated from it.

239
00:19:32,879 --> 00:19:41,644
[SPEAKER_03]: But, as I said, its roots are on the mental level, but in order to arrive at that, certain practical giving-ups need to be done.

240
00:19:43,245 --> 00:19:47,187
[SPEAKER_03]: So, like, refraining from seeking out information,

241
00:19:48,995 --> 00:19:53,319
[SPEAKER_03]: Engaging, discussing these things, they're like, yeah, but, I mean, I can, but there's no end to this.

242
00:19:53,939 --> 00:19:55,841
[SPEAKER_03]: And there's, but I must, but why?

243
00:19:55,981 --> 00:19:57,582
[SPEAKER_03]: Because you feel insecure and unsafe?

244
00:19:57,702 --> 00:20:00,164
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, does it come from the world, the lack of safety?

245
00:20:00,844 --> 00:20:03,006
[SPEAKER_03]: Or does it come from a mind that's not liberated?

246
00:20:03,146 --> 00:20:04,327
[SPEAKER_03]: You're making a choice there.

247
00:20:05,028 --> 00:20:08,210
[SPEAKER_03]: If you act towards the world, well, you got your answer.

248
00:20:08,450 --> 00:20:10,732
[SPEAKER_03]: You might claim that you are still practicing for liberation.

249
00:20:10,832 --> 00:20:11,193
[SPEAKER_03]: You're not.

250
00:20:12,153 --> 00:20:15,396
[SPEAKER_03]: You had a crossroads and you perpetually keep choosing back onto the world.

251
00:20:16,674 --> 00:20:21,499
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's exactly how you would go beyond the central domain.

252
00:20:22,099 --> 00:20:24,642
[SPEAKER_03]: Not by suppressing it, so it doesn't come up anymore.

253
00:20:25,102 --> 00:20:26,844
[SPEAKER_03]: No, but literally you stepping out.

254
00:20:26,864 --> 00:20:27,885
[SPEAKER_03]: So you see it over there.

255
00:20:28,846 --> 00:20:33,851
[SPEAKER_03]: And you see how safe you are, because that thing cannot not... it can only be over there, and you know that.

256
00:20:36,516 --> 00:20:46,592
[SPEAKER_03]: That's what it said, like, in the first jhāna, the thoughts are assailed by perceptions of sensuality that you basically withdraw yourself from, because that's kind of what's in front, but you are outside now.

257
00:20:48,037 --> 00:21:08,577
[SPEAKER_02]: So, and sometimes there are concerns to it that, you know, being withdrawn from all these talks and whatever may produce sort of an impression on others that you are avoiding them, that you are sort of

258
00:21:09,298 --> 00:21:10,459
[SPEAKER_02]: Hostile to them, maybe.

259
00:21:11,099 --> 00:21:13,460
[SPEAKER_02]: And, I mean, even in monastic environment as well.

260
00:21:13,800 --> 00:21:18,682
[SPEAKER_03]: So, basically... You have to take it on as your responsibility.

261
00:21:19,042 --> 00:21:19,762
[SPEAKER_03]: You have to accept it.

262
00:21:21,183 --> 00:21:22,904
[SPEAKER_03]: Because some people will probably see it that way.

263
00:21:24,304 --> 00:21:29,246
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you have to accept that, yeah, well, that's... And see, that's why people would keep in touch.

264
00:21:29,566 --> 00:21:30,647
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, let's touch base.

265
00:21:30,787 --> 00:21:35,789
[SPEAKER_03]: Let's just keep seeing and talking to each other every day, so we all see that we are all right.

266
00:21:35,869 --> 00:21:38,170
[SPEAKER_03]: Because, I mean, we still might be all right.

267
00:21:39,004 --> 00:21:43,087
[SPEAKER_03]: By the fact that somebody is now withdrawn from it, the mind starts thinking, what if they're not alright?

268
00:21:43,627 --> 00:21:44,828
[SPEAKER_03]: What if they're upset with me?

269
00:21:45,348 --> 00:21:48,330
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like, well, yeah, there is no end to that proliferation.

270
00:21:48,870 --> 00:21:54,273
[SPEAKER_03]: The only end to that proliferation is to recognize, okay, well, have I done anything by body, speech and mind to make them upset?

271
00:21:55,374 --> 00:21:55,554
[SPEAKER_03]: No?

272
00:21:55,754 --> 00:22:01,778
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, okay, then this mind that's trying to make me go back there to make sure nobody's upset with me, I will restrain.

273
00:22:01,858 --> 00:22:04,940
[SPEAKER_03]: I will re-establish context in regard to it until those thoughts subside.

274
00:22:06,617 --> 00:22:12,363
[SPEAKER_03]: Or, if upon reviewing you realize, oh, yeah, no, I did act a bit like this or a bit like that, well, then go and apologize, sort it out.

275
00:22:12,843 --> 00:22:23,754
[SPEAKER_03]: But still, do not use whether you feel good or bad as a criteria whether you should act, but actually use your reflection and see whether there was anything done by body speech towards them to make them upset.

276
00:22:23,874 --> 00:22:27,558
[SPEAKER_03]: If not, then you got to endure the irrational mind that's trying to get you out of the solitude.

277
00:22:28,511 --> 00:22:30,312
[SPEAKER_03]: And seek safety in your tribe.

278
00:22:31,012 --> 00:22:31,692
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's... that is...

279
00:22:32,052 --> 00:22:38,794
[SPEAKER_03]: So, the thoughts of... oh, what will... what will my peers, what will my clan, what is my family, what is my brothers...

280
00:22:39,014 --> 00:22:41,155
[SPEAKER_03]: Think of me.

281
00:22:41,175 --> 00:22:42,495
[SPEAKER_03]: Those are the factual concerns.

282
00:22:42,535 --> 00:22:46,737
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not like these random kind of abstract thoughts that a skilled monk would have.

283
00:22:46,797 --> 00:22:46,957
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

284
00:22:47,057 --> 00:22:56,980
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like on the level of sensuality and ill-will, that now you abandoned, well, what took their place are thoughts about who you are, who you were, how others see you,

285
00:22:58,367 --> 00:23:01,528
[SPEAKER_03]: Your past, your everything, oh, it's going to have that same weight.

286
00:23:02,509 --> 00:23:04,990
[SPEAKER_03]: And now you practice that right context in regard to it.

287
00:23:05,630 --> 00:23:08,452
[SPEAKER_03]: Which means it's not always going to feel pleasant.

288
00:23:08,552 --> 00:23:15,855
[SPEAKER_03]: In the same sense, practicing the context against the, you know, I don't know, central pull or acting out of a version won't feel pleasant.

289
00:23:16,953 --> 00:23:42,732
[SPEAKER_00]: That's also quite directly related to being unable to see wholesome as wholesome and unwholesome as unwholesome, because what's... measuring yourself, what I should do or what I shouldn't do, did I act wrongly or not, on the level of what do... what will my clan, my... the people I admire, the people or whatever, what will they think of me, is basically what...

290
00:23:45,533 --> 00:24:01,551
[SPEAKER_00]: In the beginning, you might need to use the advice of wise people to send you in a good direction, but at some point, you need to be able to discern for yourself what is acting wholesome, what is acting unwholesome, and not depend on basically... And when you do, those thoughts will still come.

291
00:24:01,951 --> 00:24:02,152
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

292
00:24:02,492 --> 00:24:04,393
[SPEAKER_03]: Because that's what the mind does.

293
00:24:04,493 --> 00:24:09,314
[SPEAKER_03]: It still has the habits rooted in those subtler forms of lust and aversion.

294
00:24:09,794 --> 00:24:13,995
[SPEAKER_03]: But, yeah, if you have the right reference, you won't be acting out of how those thoughts make you feel.

295
00:24:14,596 --> 00:24:16,176
[SPEAKER_03]: You won't be acting out of doubt, in other words.

296
00:24:16,536 --> 00:24:19,037
[SPEAKER_03]: You would know whether to know, I gotta do this.

297
00:24:19,903 --> 00:24:23,770
[SPEAKER_03]: And re-establish the context until it subsides on its own.

298
00:24:24,451 --> 00:24:31,825
[SPEAKER_03]: Or, oh yeah, no, okay, I should actually make some effort externally, sort the issues I created, and then go back to this.

299
00:24:31,965 --> 00:24:32,366
[SPEAKER_03]: Either way.

300
00:24:35,506 --> 00:24:49,477
[SPEAKER_02]: I remember one discussion with one monk about it, and, well, basically he was referring to the Buddha himself, talking, well, having small talks, basically, when somebody comes to him and they exchange some greeting... Yeah, pleasantries and stuff.

301
00:24:49,577 --> 00:24:51,218
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, pleasant talks.

302
00:24:52,179 --> 00:24:56,702
[SPEAKER_02]: So, how to... so, to understand where is the limit to it, so to say.

303
00:24:56,742 --> 00:24:57,442
[SPEAKER_02]: The reason behind.

304
00:24:58,343 --> 00:25:01,605
[SPEAKER_03]: See, Tekem started small talk for the other purpose.

305
00:25:01,625 --> 00:25:02,406
[SPEAKER_03]: So, he met with us.

306
00:25:02,426 --> 00:25:07,009
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's like a way of expressing sort of friendliness and then it would go into a point.

307
00:25:07,409 --> 00:25:13,973
[SPEAKER_03]: So, he wouldn't have a small talk for the sake of distraction, for the sake of non-perspective, for the sake of greed or aversion.

308
00:25:14,874 --> 00:25:17,255
[SPEAKER_03]: He wouldn't have the small talk for the sake of carelessness.

309
00:25:19,797 --> 00:25:21,738
[SPEAKER_03]: So, yeah, that's what it is.

310
00:25:23,620 --> 00:25:33,025
[SPEAKER_02]: Maybe, yeah, maybe one can say that, well, as long as one sees that these talks really produce some... Well, no.

311
00:25:33,085 --> 00:25:39,589
[SPEAKER_03]: One main thing is that as long as one realizes one is not the Buddha, not even an arahant, one will not understand how can an arahant have a small talk.

312
00:25:39,989 --> 00:25:43,492
[SPEAKER_03]: So, until then, maybe take the small talk seriously in that sense.

313
00:25:43,532 --> 00:25:48,535
[SPEAKER_03]: Do it only, like as I said, as a way of relating to others.

314
00:25:49,415 --> 00:25:52,317
[SPEAKER_03]: But not more, not less, not make a big deal out of it either.

315
00:25:53,612 --> 00:25:58,075
[SPEAKER_03]: Stop talking to me and get angry with everyone who says good morning to you or something.

316
00:25:59,175 --> 00:26:05,459
[SPEAKER_03]: But fundamentally recognize, yeah, I don't know where the line is, because if I did, I would have the right view and be on the verge of arahantship and so on.

317
00:26:06,179 --> 00:26:13,643
[SPEAKER_03]: And when I am, then I'll figure out how can the Buddha speak with kings about, you know, seemingly pointless stuff.

318
00:26:13,903 --> 00:26:15,464
[SPEAKER_03]: So, there is that order of things.

319
00:26:17,623 --> 00:26:27,370
[SPEAKER_02]: So, could the criterion be that, as long as I know that this sort of talks is sort of unwholesome to me, at least, I should refrain from them?

320
00:26:28,150 --> 00:26:29,371
[SPEAKER_03]: If you know what unwholesome is.

321
00:26:29,992 --> 00:26:31,933
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, not like, oh, I don't like this.

322
00:26:32,053 --> 00:26:33,394
[SPEAKER_03]: That doesn't necessarily mean it's unwholesome.

323
00:26:33,414 --> 00:26:39,739
[SPEAKER_02]: No, I mean, let's say, producing all these concerns and, I mean, let's say, there are some, let's say, innocent talk about politics.

324
00:26:39,799 --> 00:26:41,060
[SPEAKER_02]: I know that you are stepping in.

325
00:26:41,360 --> 00:26:41,940
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah.

326
00:26:42,080 --> 00:26:42,481
[SPEAKER_02]: And then...

327
00:26:42,781 --> 00:26:43,842
[SPEAKER_03]: I think that's an oxymoron.

328
00:26:43,862 --> 00:26:44,862
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, yeah.

329
00:26:46,343 --> 00:26:46,884
[SPEAKER_03]: No, no, sure.

330
00:26:46,904 --> 00:26:47,365
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, of course.

331
00:26:47,465 --> 00:26:52,831
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, if I don't want my mind going there, yeah, you just... you don't have to talk to it.

332
00:26:52,871 --> 00:26:56,815
[SPEAKER_03]: And if people keep insisting, then you basically stop associating with them.

333
00:26:57,856 --> 00:26:58,677
[SPEAKER_03]: It's as simple as that.

334
00:26:59,578 --> 00:27:00,960
[SPEAKER_03]: Because, yeah, you go, you know...

335
00:27:01,260 --> 00:27:07,463
[SPEAKER_03]: Just general household life, that those are the factual, real life concerns, and they should not pertain.

336
00:27:08,043 --> 00:27:15,687
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why, like, you know, even like in the suttas, you know, when monks would sort of put each other down, they would say like, oh, you can't take him seriously.

337
00:27:15,727 --> 00:27:17,308
[SPEAKER_03]: He's intimate with householders.

338
00:27:17,748 --> 00:27:19,529
[SPEAKER_03]: He has householders for his friends.

339
00:27:20,310 --> 00:27:22,671
[SPEAKER_03]: Means, pfff, he's not a proper monk.

340
00:27:22,811 --> 00:27:28,974
[SPEAKER_03]: That's all it took, having householders as your friends, because you kind of share their concerns by implication.

341
00:27:30,130 --> 00:27:33,606
[SPEAKER_03]: But, then there were cases of arahants having householders for the friends.

342
00:27:34,378 --> 00:27:37,781
[SPEAKER_03]: So, in and of itself, as I said, that's not where the root of the problem is.

343
00:27:38,281 --> 00:27:44,326
[SPEAKER_03]: But, most practically, for most people, yeah, that has to be restrained until you actually uproot it.

344
00:27:44,386 --> 00:27:48,970
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, to what extent you'll engage in those talks afterwards, you'll see.

345
00:27:49,010 --> 00:27:51,512
[SPEAKER_03]: But it won't be done for the sake of distraction or whatever else.

346
00:27:51,672 --> 00:27:52,332
[SPEAKER_03]: Or out of see.

347
00:27:52,693 --> 00:27:55,675
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, I don't really care about the politics, but I care what others think about me.

348
00:27:56,095 --> 00:27:57,156
[SPEAKER_03]: And now, they want to talk politics.

